WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:21.060
First let me just thank everyone. There's
so many great ideas. My brain is buzzing

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:25.800
with connections so I'm gonna actually
try and stick to my script here with a

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:29.040
hair off in all different directions.
So I'm gonna do a bit more reading than

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:35.910
I originally intended to do. So the first
step in fixing a problem I think is

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:40.410
identifying a problem and science has a
couple. We've talked about them today in

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:44.070
our small groups.
There's the diversity problem. The US

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:47.730
News and World Report recently noted
that the United States STEM workforce is

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:52.739
no more diverse now than it was 14 years
ago. So we're missing those insights and

00:00:52.739 --> 00:00:58.019
talents from women and minorities and
those with disabilities. They were still

00:00:58.019 --> 00:01:03.420
not participating in STEM studies or
careers. There's also a lukewarm level of

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:07.830
trust in science in the United States.
It's not getting worse though sometimes

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:11.850
people think that it's kind of staying
steady but according to a Pew Research

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:16.170
Center study only four in 10 people in
the United States report a great deal of

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:21.270
confidence in the scientific community
and it gets even more varied in terms of

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.650
response when you're talking about
issues such as climate science or

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:30.600
genetically modified food or energy
sources. Simple communication about

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:36.060
scientific facts is not the answer to
these issues. Trust in particular is

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:40.020
challenging to build as facts rub up
against political or religious beliefs

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:45.000
which are ingrained in a person's
identity and a scientific identity is

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.909
exactly what we need to build if we hope
to have groups currently

00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:52.950
underrepresented in the field study, join,
and persist in a STEM career or simply

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:57.210
gain stem literacy. This is where
conscious targeted outreach and

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:02.399
engagement to new audiences comes in. Now, this flash talk is not a place for a

00:02:02.399 --> 00:02:06.479
doctoral dissertation around the subject
so I'm going to give some quick hits to

00:02:06.479 --> 00:02:10.680
ideas which have informed the way we try
and address these issues at the Intrepid

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:17.520
Sea Air and Space Museum. So first up we
let go of the idea that the scientist or

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:23.610
the science educator knows all and no
one else has anything to offer. As was

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:26.610
referenced earlier, too often the picture
that pops up when you ask someone to

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:31.390
think scientist is male and often white, so leveraging

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:35.290
indigenous knowledge has become part of
the conversation in STEM education and

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:39.640
some research relatively recently.
Indeed the National Museum of the

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:43.269
American Indian here in New York City if
you have a chance they have just opened

00:02:43.269 --> 00:02:46.450
a new section on the math and science
practiced by Native American nations

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:50.109
historically. It's very cool and very
hands-on and it's very free because it's

00:02:50.109 --> 00:02:55.030
Smithsonian all the way downtown. In our
work at the Intrepid Sea, Air, and Space

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:59.920
Museum we think of using indigenous in
its meaning of occurring naturally

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:05.379
within a place rather than connecting to
a traditional culture. So when working to

00:03:05.379 --> 00:03:10.120
engage audiences with STEM concepts
particularly youth and families through

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:13.810
our community engagement and youth
leadership programs we provide programs

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:19.989
in and/or recruit participants from high
need areas of the city. We leave a lot of

00:03:19.989 --> 00:03:24.340
room and opportunity for participants to
become the expert and to share what they

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:29.349
already know and understand to connect
to and show off the science behind their

00:03:29.349 --> 00:03:34.599
interests, talents, and everyday practices.
Examples would be showing and sharing

00:03:34.599 --> 00:03:38.229
food preparation techniques that involve
sophisticated understanding of the

00:03:38.229 --> 00:03:42.879
fermentation process or chemical
reactions behind hair and beauty

00:03:42.879 --> 00:03:47.859
traditions. It is essential if you want
participants to develop their identity

00:03:47.859 --> 00:03:53.409
as people who engage with science daily
in a masterful way that you approach the

00:03:53.409 --> 00:03:57.939
topics with respect, encourage them to
take the lead, and look at it as a

00:03:57.939 --> 00:04:02.889
facilitated experience where we are all
learning together instead of an expert

00:04:02.889 --> 00:04:07.780
in parting knowledge from on high to the
ignorant. In short we're employing the

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:12.189
best practices of teaching and again I
would argue as Ben pointed out here that

00:04:12.189 --> 00:04:17.320
science outreach can learn a lot from
pedagogy. Aside from making sure we're

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:21.789
going to where these audiences are:
libraries, community centers, schools,

00:04:21.789 --> 00:04:26.800
public housing developments, even prisons,
when designing programs and experiences

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:30.909
we at the Museum spend a good deal of
thought on finding relevant entry points

00:04:30.909 --> 00:04:34.659
based on interests of the target
audience and our individuals within that

00:04:34.659 --> 00:04:38.770
audience and just as an aside to get
that like with NYCHA New York City

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:44.800
public housing complexes where we
developing community feedback groups so

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:48.520
again what they need, what they want, what
they understand, what they're worried

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:52.290
about in terms of bringing their
families to the museum and how we can

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:59.830
ease all those concerns and find those
relevant entry points. So having programs

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:03.190
exploring the algorithms behind
something like Spotify or the way music

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:08.230
is produced, mixed and engineered, and
recorded is a great way to engage music

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:12.310
obsessed teens for example. Connecting to
the familiar and the areas they feel

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:15.669
confident in and stretching their
thinking and experiences to new STEM

00:05:15.669 --> 00:05:20.610
concepts building scientific literacy
and once again a scientific identity.

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:24.580
Identifying these hooks and providing
connections from these interest-based

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:29.620
STEM explorations to possible STEM-based
careers is especially important for many

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:33.550
individuals from underrepresented groups
who may be the first in their family to

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:38.530
think about attending college or may
already be thinking about and working on

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:43.229
a practical level to contribute to
family support even as young as 13 or 14.

00:05:43.229 --> 00:05:47.979
Well no one doubts that being a doctor
or dentist or research scientist is a

00:05:47.979 --> 00:05:52.840
way to have an income, medical school's a
long and costly process and challenging

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:57.070
to imagine as a path for a kid whose
schools didn't have advanced maths or

00:05:57.070 --> 00:06:02.200
sciences and who at 16 isn't an A+
student in these areas. These subjects

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.200
are generally taught completely divorced
from the world these students live in

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:08.190
so they're not always successful at
them at first.

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:11.320
Technology is engaging and exciting but
we see there are challenges to

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:14.590
attracting and keeping individuals from
underrepresented groups particularly

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:19.060
women. So one of the main things I think
we do well at the Museum for all

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:23.470
audiences but particularly for our goals
for girls program is provide

00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:27.370
opportunities for them to meet and ask
questions of people who look like them

00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:32.860
at different levels in their careers
with different career paths many of

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:37.060
which do not involve working alone in a
lab or 10 years of medical training or

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:41.740
any of these other things that they
imagine goes into being a scientist or

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:46.150
working in a STEM field. In the last year
we've had an array of career and

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:49.800
mentorship Day participants such as the
first black female astronaut in space,

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:54.760
fabric engineers from Coach, chemists
from L'Oreal, and a young woman whose

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:57.820
career has had her both
figuring out those algorithms for music

00:06:57.820 --> 00:07:02.370
sales and touring as a drummer for the
artist m.i.a.

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:05.919
Best of all, in our youth leadership
programs we work with our kids

00:07:05.919 --> 00:07:10.630
explicitly on communication skills and
we hire them as paid interns or

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:15.610
eventually junior education staff. When a
new family visits our museum or a

00:07:15.610 --> 00:07:19.990
library where one of these staff members
is facilitating a program and see

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:24.030
someone who looks like them, perhaps
speaking in their home language

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:28.930
facilitating an experience that explores
a complicated scientific concept I think

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:33.100
that does more to engage new audiences
and help create a scientific identity

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:36.820
among those who hadn't seen themselves
in that light before than anything else

00:07:36.820 --> 00:07:44.200
we do. So three minutes under and I am
done

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:59.300
and while the mic goes to her I'm

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:03.760
gonna make a quick pitch. We are working
on a year-long NSF grant to try and put

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:08.080
together methods for doing mid and
longitudinal studies of girls and STEM

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:12.480
programs at museums and Science Centers. So if anybody wants to play let me know.

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:15.820
Thanks for that talk, Lynda - I'm a big fan
of your work.

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:18.970
One of the questions that I continue to
kind of wrestle with and when you

00:08:18.970 --> 00:08:23.110
mentioned putting scientists in front of
students who look like them is how we

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:27.550
continue to ask for volunteers or not
necessarily volunteers even paid

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:31.840
professionals to come in and give their
time and work to be the demonstration of

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:35.620
someone who looks like you can be a
scientist while at the same time not

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:39.250
asking that of typically their white
male peers. So how do we continue to

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:45.070
justify using the time of current
scientists while leaving white males at

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:49.690
the bench to continue to pursue their
careers without interruption and how can

00:08:49.690 --> 00:08:54.000
we balance that in a way that might be
beneficial to all parties?

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:57.780
That's a great question and we don't leave them on the
bench we get them out for other things

00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:03.320
for like public panels and things like
that so we definitely make use of them -

00:09:03.329 --> 00:09:11.290
absolutely. I think that for for the
women that we asked for particularly for

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:16.240
the goals program a lot of them are
coming from industry so unlike academia

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:20.740
they actually they get paid days to go
volunteer. Like that's part of the

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:25.420
structure of their environment and they
realize it was such a struggle for them

00:09:25.420 --> 00:09:30.190
very much being the only girl in
their coding class at school, the only

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:35.800
woman in their college program, or one of
five or something that this idea of

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:38.890
building a network with these young
women it's a ten year old program now so

00:09:38.890 --> 00:09:45.730
it's nice it's got a whole sort of peer
network going on is is really at their

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:49.180
heart. So I think it's something they
would do even if they weren't

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:53.350
compensated for it but it's much easier
for people who are not in academia

00:09:53.350 --> 00:09:59.770
because academia doesn't always honor a
deeper work with towards tenure-track so

00:09:59.770 --> 00:10:09.760
for the younger staff, the younger
faculty it is difficult.

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:15.100
If you followed them on Twitter in various other
communities where their response would

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:19.300
be how dare not ask us this is the work
that we that we understand that we're

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:23.140
supposed to be doing because we are at
this level and we understand what is

00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:28.000
behind us and we have to pay it forward.
Many of the groups that we work with at

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:32.260
UNC Chapel Hill I'm one of those it's
the creator of the Twitter handle blackinSTEM

00:10:32.260 --> 00:10:35.770
that is part of her work who
she is when she takes on a postdoc

00:10:35.770 --> 00:10:39.820
position is understood that I'm going to
be doing science outreach for this

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:43.480
purpose, for this topic and so many
others in the community as well whether

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:48.070
we're talking about astrophysicists or
astronomers like Judea Eisler or Daniel

00:10:48.070 --> 00:10:51.820
Lee some of those it is part of their
work and understood. So I think they're

00:10:51.820 --> 00:10:59.680
creating a culture where many many who
who are underrepresented scientists in

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:04.839
their field understand that this is part
of what what is required of us what is

00:11:04.839 --> 00:11:09.970
needed to to change the landscape in the
future. So I don't think for many of them

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:13.990
not saying all but it's not asking it's
not stretching saying that why leaving

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:19.960
out white males but it's saying that
this is work that we must we must do.

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:27.360
I actually want to echo that because and
also say that for two years I did work

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:31.840
with the goals for girls at the Intrepid
Sea so and I remember when I was

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:36.340
reaching out to a something I was a grad
student and I luckily had a PI who was

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:39.730
very generous with how often I really
needed to be in the lab and so for me it

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:43.600
wasn't necessarily am I going to get
paid to do this it was how can I not do

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:48.280
this and how can I actually expose
myself A to these students but then

00:11:48.280 --> 00:11:51.520
also to other individuals at the
Intrepid so that I can get more

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:57.130
opportunitie-s to do this because maybe
while you know white counterparts maybe

00:11:57.130 --> 00:12:00.730
the reason they can say no is because
they get asked all the time to do these

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:05.680
things and so for these opportunities I
took them as much as I could because I

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:10.600
saw it as paying it forward. So I just
wanted to echo what you were saying because

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:14.320
I actually had a conversation with Jade
Ida about this very thing and that was

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:18.580
the thing that she said that she saw it
not as an extra thing but as an

00:12:18.580 --> 00:12:22.980
extension of what she was already doing.

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:30.060
I'll make this comment quick.  My name's Sarah McAnulty. I run Skype a Scientist and

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:36.640
one nice thing then when we're like planning big scale outreach events or programs, one thing that we

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:40.480
focus on is making sure that the
minorities that we do have don't get

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:47.860
like quote-unquote wasted on white kids.
So so like not so that we make sure that

00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:52.000
we're making use of every person to
their best ability and when you're

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:55.420
planning one of these programs that are
huge that's something that you should

00:12:55.420 --> 00:12:59.020
like just for all of us in the room to
like keep in mind. And there are also a

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:05.350
lot of underrepresented groups in the
u.s. that a white dude is awesome for

00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:10.150
like a rural community that wouldn't see
a scientist otherwise there and we never

00:13:10.150 --> 00:13:14.170
don't want the white dudes participating
we absolutely do and we can use every

00:13:14.170 --> 00:13:18.070
single one of them but we want to make
sure they're going to the right place

00:13:18.070 --> 00:13:22.270
and if you have like big algorithms to
sort of help get people to where they're

00:13:22.270 --> 00:13:26.540
best used, that's really powerful.

